WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.487 --> 00:00:02.787
- Welcome back!

00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.787
- I'm so happy to be here.
- Go ahead. Go ahead.

00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:10.424
- Hi rad moms. Wow.

00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:12.313
- Go ahead. It's all
right. It's all right.

00:00:12.313 --> 00:00:14.550
Do you get that just
when you enter Target,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.087
like you go and people
are just like, "Woo!"

00:00:17.087 --> 00:00:18.510
- I wish.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.310
- So wait, do you get into Christmas?

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.750
- Oh, I love, love Christmas.
It is my favorite holiday.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:25.583
- Me too.

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:26.416
- I love it.

00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:29.940
And we usually spend Christmas in Hawaii.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.076
- Which is kind of different because I,

00:00:32.076 --> 00:00:32.940
not very snowy.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.490
- Not very snowy, but nice.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.820
It's where Barack is from. So
that's where his family is.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.140
And because it's so far away,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.810
it's really the time we
get to spend with them.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.190
So it's become a tradition.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.023
- I love Hawaii.

00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:46.530
Do you love Hawaii?
- I mean, that was half

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.080
the reason why I said yes.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.330
(crowd laughs)
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.107
- What was-
- Like, in laws. Beach. Sun.

00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:55.500
I was like, "I think I love you."

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.333
- Got lucky there.

00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:59.340
So wait, before the show we
asked our rad mom viewers

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.320
if they had any questions for Mrs. Obama.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.150
So let's do one of those right now.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.600
So Alicia from Minneapolis
asks, "Before we had kids,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.260
my husband and I agreed to
share the work," (chuckles)

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.797
oh, sorry. (laughs)

00:01:11.797 --> 00:01:14.400
"To share the work of parenting 50/50."

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.660
- Oh see, we all laughed.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.280
You could barely even get that out.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.905
Like ha ha ha. Silly men.

00:01:20.905 --> 00:01:22.830
- "Both of us have careers.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.130
Let me tell you, it's
70/30 on a good day."

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.247
I think you're winning.

00:01:27.247 --> 00:01:29.970
"I realize this man will
probably never change,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.490
which is frustrating because
he's truly a good dad

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.510
and husband."

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.430
What is your advice on how to manage

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.263
my feelings about this?"

00:01:36.263 --> 00:01:38.257
I feel like we should just
send her Elsa singing,

00:01:38.257 --> 00:01:39.457
"Let it Go."

00:01:39.457 --> 00:01:42.040
(all laughing)

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.510
'Cause like, you know what?
- But that's great.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.770
You know, that's great advice.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:47.880
We have all been through it.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.220
That whole 50/50, I
write in the book about-

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.060
- That doesn't exist I don't think.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.763
- It doesn't exist.
And it's not realistic.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.130
What I would say is-

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.390
- You're busier at different times too.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.690
- Exactly. I mean has it
been 50/50 in moments?

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.320
No. Has it been 50/50
for me over 30 years?

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.780
Yes. It balances out.
- Ooh, I like that. Yeah.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.740
- So what my advice would be,
make sure you are taking care

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.740
of yourself, you know, because
that's the thing that we do.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.930
Sometimes we take on more

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.980
because we feel guilty when we go work out

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.933
or when we go to the hairdresser.

00:02:22.885 --> 00:02:26.553
And we also don't trust him
with our kids, you know?

00:02:26.553 --> 00:02:29.580
- Will they arrive in tact
without a broken bone?

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.610
- So we have to, like you said, let it go.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.900
My advice, when I stopped
resenting my husband for that,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.630
was when I started prioritizing myself,

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.100
and I didn't wait for him.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.880
So the help I learned didn't
have to come from him,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.840
but I needed it.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.630
So I relied on my mom and my girlfriends

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.800
and a broader community of
people to give me that break

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.110
so that I'm not so mad
at him all the time.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.640
- I think that that's
good too though as well

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.250
because a village is
raising my kids with me.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.990
You know, whether it's
grandma, whether it's my,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.700
you know, sister, whether it's my nanny,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.100
whether it's my people
around me, my friends.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.110
Like I think a village is-
- Why we've gotten out of

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.030
that habit, that notion.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.580
We're making moms feel like they have

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.900
to do it all.
- Do everything, yeah.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.310
- And it was never like that.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.590
I mean we're really the first generation

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.120
of the nuclear family.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.890
It's just like me, my husband and my kids,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.630
or my partner and my kids.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.510
That was never the case.
I was raised in community.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.060
I was raised with a great-aunt downstairs.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:27.893
- I was raised in daycare.
- Grandparents around

00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:28.890
the corner.
- Yeah.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.790
- You know, you have to rely
on a community of people

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:33.690
to make it happen.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.460
That's the way you raise good kids,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.120
and they get benefit from being
exposed to different people.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.130
Different, you know, approaches.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.390
- Find more independence.
- Exactly.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.760
So don't try to do it
on your own, you know?

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.080
Do it with him.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.013
Get his 30 and get the
other 50 from other people.

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:54.181
- And then listen to
"Let it Go" in your head.

00:03:54.181 --> 00:03:56.700
Her new book is called
"The Light We Carry."

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.640
So you've referred to your new
book as a toolbox of sorts,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.320
which the only place I've
ever heard this is either

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.270
at my kids' school, it's a
great school my kids go to,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.700
or in therapy because
it's something for, yeah,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.440
because it's something
for your toolbox to learn.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.240
It's like, obviously I
haven't learned this lesson

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.680
'cause it keeps hitting me in the face.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.690
Like, what does it mean
for you? Like a toolbox.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.560
What does that mean?

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.090
- For me, it's just the habits

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.180
and practices that I've accumulated

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.460
over the course of my 58 years of life

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.120
that get me through
uncertainty and tough times,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.730
which we have all been going through.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.250
And I get asked a lot
as a former first lady,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.200
as somebody who's out
there as a public figure

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.777
as I assure you are,
especially from young people,

00:04:34.777 --> 00:04:36.510
"How do I find my voice?"

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.343
You know,

00:04:38.396 --> 00:04:39.229
"How am I seen?"
- Because sometimes you're

00:04:39.229 --> 00:04:40.440
afraid to say something.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.370
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.030
- Exactly. People wanna know
how do you find a good partner?

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.293
How do I parent?

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.080
This book is my offering.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.080
But I also want this to
be, this book to lead

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.710
to a conversation of other mothers, women,

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:58.050
people sharing how they get through

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.040
because uncertainty is baked
into the human experience.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.980
And I think too much
time is spent trying not

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.440
to feel anxious or feel uncertainty.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.273
- Filter yourself,

00:05:08.273 --> 00:05:09.540
like an Instagram.
- To filter yourself.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.030
When the truth is you gotta just lean

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.130
into the fact that that's part of life.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.580
So now, how do we figure
out how to manage it?

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.520
You know, how do we get
through without falling apart?

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.870
- I love what you just said too,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.850
'cause I feel like it's kind
of a dedication in the book

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.260
that ignited the light in you, right?

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.580
I feel like that's what
you're trying to do

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.320
for others is ignite their light.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.210
- And everybody has that light.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.760
And I think in times like
this where we feel divided

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.400
and the messaging out there is negative,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.550
we start to think there's
no light out there.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.000
But my experience as the
former first lady living

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.120
in this country is everybody's
got that light, you know?

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.310
And over time, it's either fueled,

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.690
especially when we're kids.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.450
It's fueled by love and
support and investment.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:55.080
Or it can be slowly snuffed out by hatred

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.430
and bigotry and lack of-

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:57.420
- You just get beaten down.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.830
- They just get beaten down.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.320
But the light is there. It's in all of us.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.960
- I feel like too, it's about small things

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.970
because I feel like the
world can be overwhelming.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.190
Even like this show,
we have such a stage to

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.780
help so many people, but
it can be overwhelming.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.920
There's so much need.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.540
There's so many people
doing amazing things.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:13.860
But it can get overwhelming.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.860
Especially watching the news
can be very just soul crushing.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.870
- It could be paralyzing.
- Yeah. Just soul crushing.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.340
And you're like, you
kind of feel hopeless.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.980
But I feel like it's the small things

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.510
and I feel like you
touch on this too, right?

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.830
- It's one of the chapters,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.560
it's called The Power of Small.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.393
- The small little ripples-

00:06:32.393 --> 00:06:34.230
- I give that advice to a lot

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:37.290
of young kids who feel
overwhelmed by the bigness

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.350
of the problems we're
facing and they're facing.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.890
What I've learned to do
is to control what I can.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.840
And throughout the stages of my life,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.600
that power and control
has varied, you know?

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.780
What I was able to control
at 15 is very different

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:52.890
from what I can control

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.050
as the first lady of the United States.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.740
But what I could do was
what I had control over.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.060
And sometimes that helps us to prevent

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.700
from making great the enemy of the good.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.190
Sometimes if we feel like
we can't fix everything

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.770
then we feel like we
shouldn't do anything at all.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.900
And I don't want young
people to get burned out

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.040
especially as they're thinking

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.380
about big problems like climate change

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.090
and criminal justice and
racism and global economy.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:21.923
It's like-
- That's what I'm saying.

00:07:21.923 --> 00:07:23.790
It could be overwhelming.
But maybe find a focus.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:24.757
- You're 15, you know?

00:07:24.757 --> 00:07:28.080
If you're 15, your focus should be school.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.850
You know, do your homework.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.380
That's what you can control.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.090
You know, don't bite off so much

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.180
before you have the ability
to really handle it.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:38.820
So there's a whole chapter on that.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.410
That's an important tool of mine.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:44.910
Even in my position, I
stay hopeful because I look

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.090
at the thing right in front
of me that I can actually do.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.040
You know, we can't fix climate change

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.480
if we're not gonna vote.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.483
And voting is that small
thing that we all control.

00:07:54.483 --> 00:07:55.590
- [Kelly] It's a big thing.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:56.730
- That is a big thing.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.520
That's the power of how small it is.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.966
- [Kelly] Power of the people. Absolutely.

00:08:00.966 --> 00:08:01.799
(crowd cheers)

00:08:01.799 --> 00:08:02.640
So in this new book,

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.520
you say your girls are in the
flea market stage of life.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:06.630
So what is that?

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.300
- That's a metaphor also for watching

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.420
your adult children start to
create home for themselves.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.253
- Yeah.

00:08:13.253 --> 00:08:14.400
- So we went by their apartment,

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.490
Barack and I, for cocktails before dinner.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:18.450
- Oh my gosh!

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.550
- And they had made a
couple of weak martinis,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.160
which I was glad to know
they didn't really know how

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:23.993
to make martinis.

00:08:23.993 --> 00:08:25.500
- Like, did you put alcohol in it?

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.090
- Yeah, yeah. It was bad.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.160
But it was a good effort. Good effort.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.490
So they had the nerve to
then as we put down drinks

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.380
and be like, "Let me get a coaster."

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.667
And I was like, oh, all of a sudden-

00:08:35.667 --> 00:08:37.560
- Oh, do we care about rings right now?

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:38.970
- We care about coasters.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.370
You care about your
little $4 coffee table.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.134
- I know.
- You know?

00:08:43.134 --> 00:08:45.420
You didn't care about
coasters in the White House

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.590
for all eight years.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:49.260
I didn't see you rushing
to protect that antique

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.820
surface.
- It's so true.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.170
'Cause I started making my kids,

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:53.003
I take 'em to Target, I'm like,

00:08:53.003 --> 00:08:56.010
"I'm gonna show you what toys
cost 'cause you're unaware."

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.570
So I make them and I said, oh,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.005
they're like, "We have
$20, let's go get it."

00:08:59.005 --> 00:09:00.805
And I go, "Yeah, let's go with your $20

00:09:00.805 --> 00:09:02.490
and see what you're gonna get."

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.533
And they were so disappointed,

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:07.110
because everything is so expensive.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.340
And I'm like, welcome.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.550
- That's their whole, you
know, they're very cheap now

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.170
with their own money, which I appreciate.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.250
- With ours though, they're not.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.480
With my wallet, they're fine. (laughs)

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.127
- That's right. That's right.

00:09:17.127 --> 00:09:19.770
So, you know, cobbling
together an apartment,

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.830
building a life, creating
their own friendships,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.890
watching them build their own community,

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.170
it's like, better than I expected.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.360
I mean, I miss having them with me,

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.420
but just watching them become real people

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.340
in the world and doing okay-

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.173
- [Kelly] And adulting.

00:09:36.173 --> 00:09:38.220
- Adulting as they say. It's like-

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.270
- I can't imagine my kids,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:40.230
like they're six and eight so.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.840
- And you know, you don't need
to, they're not ready yet.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:44.010
- [Kelly] The world isn't ready yet.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.920
- They're not ready yet.
That's why they're with you.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.570
- Another thing that a
lot of us mamas deal with

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.190
is making friends as an adult.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:51.023
- Yeah.

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:51.960
- And I love that you refer to this.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.550
Is it the kitchen table?

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:54.383
- [Michelle] Yeah.

00:09:54.383 --> 00:09:55.770
- So what is, explain
what the kitchen table.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.090
I love this. That's cool.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:00.450
- I call it the kitchen
table because growing up

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.190
everything happened in
the kitchen, you know?

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:03.023
- Yeah.

00:10:03.023 --> 00:10:04.500
- Because you come to your kitchen table

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.020
for safety and love and security, right?

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:08.040
So I called it that.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.240
But for me, it's my friendship
group outside of my family.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.850
I still have my mom, my family crew.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:19.380
But we particularly as women,
I find that my kitchen table,

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:22.020
those are the other people that stop me

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:26.130
from being mad at the 30%
my husband is giving me.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:30.540
Because they're stepping up
and giving me what he cannot.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:34.050
And I want my girls to practice
developing a kitchen table.

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:37.260
That's a tool. The kitchen
table is a tool, right?

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.060
And I experienced that as first lady.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.610
I mean, I had to make
new friends because I

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.620
was still a mother raising kids.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.100
And rad moms know, you've
got to have mother friends

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:47.933
in the school.

00:10:47.933 --> 00:10:49.740
- Yeah.
- So I had to step out.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:52.020
Nobody was gonna come up to me, you know?

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.990
You couldn't even get
near me with security.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:56.882
- I was like, they're
probably scared. (laughs)

00:10:56.882 --> 00:10:58.530
- They were, yeah, they
were probably scared.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:01.110
So I tell the story of
the first time I set

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.400
up a play date for Sasha,

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.290
because I just had to find a mother that I

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:05.370
thought I wanted to meet.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.710
- I just love that
you're doing a play date.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:09.990
- We set up a play date.
- I can't imagine that call.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:11.940
- She is now a good friend of mine,

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.747
but at first I just
saw her and I was like,

00:11:13.747 --> 00:11:16.950
"Why don't you have
little Olivia come over?"

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:18.600
And of course she was like, "Sure!"

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.660
And so she, later as we
became friends, she told me

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:24.480
the story of what it was
like coming to pick Olivia up

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.520
after that, coming to the White House,

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:27.843
which you know,

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.140
what is on the other
side of being my friend?

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.630
Well, it is a social security check.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.690
It's, you know-
- Background check.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.993
- It's a clear vet before you
can even come in the gate.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.320
Not to mention she wasn't
even coming in the house,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.230
but she went and got her
hair done, her nails done,

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.190
washed her car.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.610
- She was like, "Yeah!"
- She was like,

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.390
"I am not showing up
messy at the White House."

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.170
And so she pulls in and I decide outside

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:58.110
of protocol that I'm
gonna take little Olivia

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.090
down to the south lawn.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:02.760
Which you do not leave the
residence as a principal

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:05.910
because it sets off all
this outside security.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.580
The counter assault team
goes into action high gear.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:11.040
- Oh my God.
- Black flack jackets

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.250
with machine guns, dogs,
they start descending.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:15.720
- Yeah.
- So here I come.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.020
Her car is now surrounded by security.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.760
I step out into the south lawn

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.760
with little Olivia and I'm
like, "Get out. Come on out."

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.497
But she had been explicitly told,

00:12:26.497 --> 00:12:30.660
"Under no circumstances, ma'am, are you to

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:32.100
get out of your car."

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.270
- This woman is about to

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:34.103
pee her pants.
- So I'm calling her.

00:12:34.103 --> 00:12:35.160
I'm like, "Get out."

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:36.360
And she's like (groans).

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.900
So she slowly gets out to carefully,

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.880
and we have a great chat.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.100
She played it all off. I
didn't know any of this.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:48.410
So I'm retelling her story.

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.220
- Oh, this is a nice woman.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.560
- And she is now one of my best friends.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.080
Olivia's like a daughter to me, you know?

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.510
Lo and behold a new daisy popped up

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.340
and it is a beautiful thing.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.760
And if I hadn't taken
the risk to extend myself

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:06.510
and to reach out to a stranger, you know,

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.180
and make a friend, she
wouldn't be in my life.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:14.040
And so if I can make new
friends, anybody can, all right?

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.623
- 'Cause there's people that, (laughs)

00:13:17.623 --> 00:13:19.740
oh my God, we need another break.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.680
But first, Mrs. Obama brought
an early Christmas gift

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:23.513
for everyone here.

00:13:23.513 --> 00:13:25.957
You are all going home
with a copy of her new book

00:13:25.957 --> 00:13:29.587
"The Light We Carry."
(audience cheers)

